# The Evolution of Mobile Development

## The Native Mobile Era

By the end of the previous chapter, we understood the engineering problem that Kotlin Multiplatform was designed to solve.

Before we explore how Kotlin Multiplatform works internally, it's important to understand **how the mobile industry reached this point**.

Software architecture doesn't evolve by accident.

Every major shift in software engineering is usually a response to a limitation in the previous generation of technology.

Kotlin Multiplatform is no exception.

To understand why KMP exists, we first need to travel back to the early days of mobile application development.

---

# The Beginning of Modern Mobile Development

In 2007, Apple introduced the first iPhone.

A year later, Google launched Android.

These two events completely changed the software industry.

For the first time, smartphones became powerful enough to run sophisticated applications.

Companies quickly realized that mobile devices were becoming the primary way customers interacted with digital products.

Businesses that previously focused on websites suddenly needed mobile applications.

Banks wanted banking apps.

Retailers wanted shopping apps.

Restaurants wanted food delivery apps.

Healthcare providers wanted patient portals.

Every business wanted a presence on mobile devices.

This demand created an entirely new software ecosystem.

---

# The Rise of Native Applications

In the early years, there was only one practical approach to mobile development:

**Build native applications.**

If you wanted to build an Android application, you used Android technologies.

If you wanted an iPhone application, you used Apple's technologies.

There was no concept of sharing business logic.

There was no cross-platform architecture.

Each platform was treated as an independent product.

```
                  Mobile Product

             "Build Android App"

                     │

               Android Team

                     │

           Java / Android SDK

                     │

              Android APK
```

```
                  Mobile Product

              "Build iOS App"

                     │

                 iOS Team

                     │

          Objective-C / Cocoa

                     │

                iPhone App
```

This approach quickly became the industry standard.

---

# Why Native Development Became the Standard

Native development offered several important advantages.

The operating system provided everything developers needed.

Android applications had direct access to:

- Camera
- GPS
- Bluetooth
- Sensors
- Notifications
- Storage
- Background Services

Similarly, iOS applications could use:

- Camera
- Face ID
- Touch ID
- Core Location
- Keychain
- Push Notifications
- UIKit

Developers weren't fighting the platform.

They were building directly on top of it.

As a result, applications felt fast.

Animations were smooth.

Scrolling was responsive.

User interfaces followed each platform's design language.

For many years, native development represented the gold standard for mobile applications.

---

# Native Development Was Never the Problem

When developers hear discussions about Kotlin Multiplatform or Flutter, they sometimes assume native development had failed.

It didn't.

Native development solved exactly the problem it was designed to solve.

It enabled developers to build applications that felt completely integrated with the operating system.

Consider a simple Android application.

```
   User
    │
    ▼
 Activity
    │
    ▼
ViewModel
    │
    ▼
Repository
    │
    ▼
 Network
    │
    ▼
  Server
```

Every layer worked naturally with Android.

Lifecycle management was built into the framework.

Permissions were handled by the operating system.

Background execution followed Android's lifecycle.

Everything fit together.

The same was true on iOS.

Native development wasn't broken.

The challenge appeared somewhere else.

---

# The Smartphone Explosion

As smartphone adoption accelerated, customer expectations changed.

Businesses no longer asked:

> "Should we build a mobile application?"

Instead, they asked:

> "When will the Android version be ready?"

Immediately followed by:

> "When will the iPhone version be available?"

Supporting multiple platforms quickly became a business requirement rather than a technical decision.

The engineering organization suddenly looked like this.

```
                Product Team
                      │
             New Mobile Feature
                      │
        ┌─────────────┴─────────────┐
        ▼                           ▼
   Android Team                iOS Team
```

Initially, this seemed perfectly reasonable.

Each team specialized in its own platform.

Each team built native applications.

Each team delivered excellent user experiences.

No one questioned the architecture.

